1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:03,204
Imagine a table of data with one hundred columns.

2
00:00:03,643 --> 00:00:05,718
It looks like one hundred different directions.

3
00:00:06,143 --> 00:00:08,550
But some columns may only repeat others.

4
00:00:08,949 --> 00:00:14,454
A length in meters and the same length in centimeters say the same thing twice.

5
00:00:14,906 --> 00:00:16,582
So how many directions are really there?

6
00:00:16,994 --> 00:00:21,356
That number has a name, the rank, and today we learn to count it.

7
00:00:22,259 --> 00:00:31,554
You will learn when vectors are redundant, what a basis is, how to describe one point with two different bases, and how to find the rank of a matrix.

8
00:00:31,993 --> 00:00:37,831
You need video one, about vectors and span, and video two, about matrices.

9
00:00:39,573 --> 00:00:41,448
In video one we met a warning.

10
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:45,743
The vectors two, one and four, two point along the same line.

11
00:00:46,221 --> 00:00:48,908
Four, two is just two times two, one.

12
00:00:49,333 --> 00:00:52,711
Together they only reach a line, not the whole plane.

13
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:55,796
The second vector adds nothing new.

14
00:00:56,221 --> 00:00:57,299
We say it is redundant.

15
00:00:58,239 --> 00:00:59,795
Redundancy can also hide.

16
00:01:00,221 --> 00:01:01,949
Take three vectors in the plane.

17
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:03,838
The vector a is two, one.

18
00:01:04,276 --> 00:01:06,125
The vector b is one, two.

19
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:09,104
The vector c is three, three.

20
00:01:09,542 --> 00:01:11,284
No two of them are parallel.

21
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:13,106
So is each one really needed?

22
00:01:14,073 --> 00:01:14,791
Look closer.

23
00:01:15,216 --> 00:01:17,450
Walk along a, and then along b.

24
00:01:17,889 --> 00:01:21,479
You arrive at three, three, which is exactly c.

25
00:01:21,905 --> 00:01:23,421
So c equals a plus b.

26
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:28,128
Anything you can build with c, you can already build with a and b.

27
00:01:28,567 --> 00:01:30,908
The vector c brings no new direction.

28
00:01:31,826 --> 00:01:38,275
So the span of a, b and c is the same as the span of a and b alone: the whole plane.

29
00:01:38,714 --> 00:01:42,278
We have three vectors, but only two real directions.

30
00:01:42,717 --> 00:01:48,049
We need a test that finds this kind of hidden redundancy, even in a hundred dimensions.

31
00:01:49,786 --> 00:01:51,009
Here is the test.

32
00:01:51,435 --> 00:01:58,456
A set of vectors is called linearly independent if no vector in the set is a linear combination of the others.

33
00:01:58,882 --> 00:02:01,661
Then each vector adds a new direction.

34
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:07,538
If at least one vector can be built from the others, the set is linearly dependent.

35
00:02:08,473 --> 00:02:12,954
There is an equivalent way to say it, and it is easier to check.

36
00:02:13,379 --> 00:02:17,728
Look for weights that make the linear combination equal to the zero vector.

37
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:20,095
All-zero weights always work.

38
00:02:20,534 --> 00:02:23,552
The vectors are independent when that is the only way.

39
00:02:23,978 --> 00:02:27,116
If other weights also give zero, they are dependent.

40
00:02:28,073 --> 00:02:30,160
Read the formula symbol by symbol.

41
00:02:30,573 --> 00:02:32,208
Lambda is a Greek letter.

42
00:02:32,674 --> 00:02:35,998
Here, lambda i is a weight, a scalar.

43
00:02:36,424 --> 00:02:38,817
And x i is the i-th vector.

44
00:02:39,256 --> 00:02:41,743
The big sum adds up the weighted vectors.

45
00:02:42,182 --> 00:02:48,737
The arrow says: if this sum is the zero vector, then every lambda must be zero.

46
00:02:50,259 --> 00:02:54,195
Example one: a, b and c from before.

47
00:02:54,621 --> 00:02:57,520
Take the weights one, one, and minus one.

48
00:02:57,959 --> 00:03:02,613
The first components give two plus one minus three, which is zero.

49
00:03:03,052 --> 00:03:07,773
The second components give one plus two minus three, which is zero.

50
00:03:08,198 --> 00:03:13,916
Walk along a, then b, then back along c, and you return to the start.

51
00:03:14,355 --> 00:03:19,568
Non-zero weights give zero, so a, b and c are dependent.

52
00:03:21,093 --> 00:03:24,058
Example two: only a and b.

53
00:03:24,603 --> 00:03:31,226
Suppose lambda one times two, one plus lambda two times one, two is the zero vector.

54
00:03:31,664 --> 00:03:36,957
The first components give two lambda one plus lambda two equals zero.

55
00:03:37,382 --> 00:03:40,986
So lambda two equals minus two lambda one.

56
00:03:41,919 --> 00:03:47,784
Put that into the second components: lambda one plus two lambda two equals zero.

57
00:03:48,209 --> 00:03:53,688
That becomes lambda one minus four lambda one, which is minus three lambda one.

58
00:03:54,113 --> 00:03:58,701
So lambda one is zero, and then lambda two is zero too.

59
00:03:59,127 --> 00:04:03,023
Only the zero weights work, so a and b are independent.

60
00:04:04,539 --> 00:04:05,417
Two quick facts.

61
00:04:05,829 --> 00:04:12,677
Any set that contains the zero vector is dependent, because one times the zero vector is already zero.

62
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:20,364
And a set where one vector is a multiple of another, like two, one and four, two, is dependent too.

63
00:04:22,106 --> 00:04:25,377
Now we can say exactly what a good set of building blocks is.

64
00:04:25,789 --> 00:04:31,999
A basis of a vector space is a set of vectors that is linearly independent and spans the whole space.

65
00:04:32,425 --> 00:04:37,864
It is a smallest spanning set: remove any vector and you lose part of the space.

66
00:04:39,373 --> 00:04:48,162
The most familiar basis of the plane is e one, equal to one, zero, and e two, equal to zero, one.

67
00:04:48,588 --> 00:04:52,338
They are independent, and every point is a combination of them.

68
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:57,444
The point one, three is one times e one plus three times e two.

69
00:04:57,883 --> 00:05:00,623
We say its coordinates are one and three.

70
00:05:01,539 --> 00:05:03,281
But a basis is not unique.

71
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:04,731
Here is another one.

72
00:05:05,156 --> 00:05:10,103
The vector b one is one, one, and b two is minus one, one.

73
00:05:10,542 --> 00:05:15,263
They are not parallel, so they are independent, and together they span the plane.

74
00:05:15,688 --> 00:05:19,877
The new grid is turned and stretched, but it still covers everything.

75
00:05:20,793 --> 00:05:24,769
Now describe the same point, one, three, with the new basis.

76
00:05:25,194 --> 00:05:33,293
We need weights c one and c two, so that c one times b one plus c two times b two equals one, three.

77
00:05:33,731 --> 00:05:38,266
The first components say: c one minus c two equals one.

78
00:05:38,705 --> 00:05:43,239
The second components say: c one plus c two equals three.

79
00:05:44,159 --> 00:05:45,502
Add the two equations.

80
00:05:45,928 --> 00:05:49,146
Two c one equals four, so c one is two.

81
00:05:49,558 --> 00:05:50,954
Then c two is one.

82
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:51,792
Check it.

83
00:05:52,231 --> 00:05:54,412
Two times one, one is two, two.

84
00:05:54,824 --> 00:05:58,268
Add minus one, one, and you get one, three.

85
00:05:58,707 --> 00:06:03,308
So in the new basis, the same point has the coordinates two and one.

86
00:06:04,239 --> 00:06:06,580
Same point, two different descriptions.

87
00:06:07,032 --> 00:06:09,479
The coordinates depend on the basis you choose.

88
00:06:09,917 --> 00:06:16,872
And because basis vectors are independent, each point has exactly one set of coordinates in a given basis.

89
00:06:17,311 --> 00:06:21,433
With redundant vectors, one point could have many descriptions.

90
00:06:22,986 --> 00:06:24,076
Here is a deep fact.

91
00:06:24,502 --> 00:06:27,853
Every basis of a space has the same number of vectors.

92
00:06:28,292 --> 00:06:29,941
This number is called the dimension.

93
00:06:30,366 --> 00:06:31,909
The plane has dimension two.

94
00:06:32,348 --> 00:06:37,281
Ordinary space, with length, width and height, has dimension three.

95
00:06:37,707 --> 00:06:41,523
That is also why three vectors in the plane are always dependent.

96
00:06:43,059 --> 00:06:44,575
Careful with a common mistake.

97
00:06:45,014 --> 00:06:48,498
Dimension is not the number of entries in each vector.

98
00:06:48,937 --> 00:06:52,634
The span of the single vector zero, one is a line.

99
00:06:53,073 --> 00:06:56,783
Its vectors have two entries, but the line has dimension one.

100
00:06:57,195 --> 00:07:03,245
In the same way, a flat plane through the origin in three-dimensional space has dimension two.

101
00:07:04,959 --> 00:07:06,422
Now back to matrices.

102
00:07:06,861 --> 00:07:11,209
The rank of a matrix is the number of linearly independent columns.

103
00:07:11,635 --> 00:07:16,209
It answers our question: how many real directions do the columns contain?

104
00:07:16,635 --> 00:07:22,020
All the combinations of the columns form the column space, and its dimension is the rank.

105
00:07:23,539 --> 00:07:24,297
Example one.

106
00:07:24,736 --> 00:07:33,659
This three by three matrix has the columns one, two, zero, then minus one, minus two, zero, then three, six, zero.

107
00:07:34,098 --> 00:07:37,382
The second column is minus one times the first.

108
00:07:37,821 --> 00:07:40,574
The third column is three times the first.

109
00:07:40,986 --> 00:07:43,260
So only one column is independent.

110
00:07:43,712 --> 00:07:47,595
The rank is one, and the column space is just a line.

111
00:07:49,113 --> 00:07:49,924
Example two.

112
00:07:50,376 --> 00:07:55,376
The columns are one, zero, one, then zero, one, one, then one, one, two.

113
00:07:55,801 --> 00:07:59,565
The first two columns are not parallel, so they are independent.

114
00:08:00,004 --> 00:08:03,315
But the third column is the first plus the second.

115
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:09,179
So the rank is two, and the column space is a flat plane through the origin.

116
00:08:10,693 --> 00:08:17,169
And the three by three identity matrix has three independent columns, so its rank is three.

117
00:08:17,594 --> 00:08:20,653
Its columns reach all of three-dimensional space.

118
00:08:21,092 --> 00:08:25,440
The rank can never be larger than the number of rows or the number of columns.

119
00:08:25,879 --> 00:08:30,413
When it is as large as possible, we say the matrix has full rank.

120
00:08:31,939 --> 00:08:33,761
One more surprising fact from the book.

121
00:08:34,187 --> 00:08:39,173
The number of independent rows always equals the number of independent columns.

122
00:08:39,612 --> 00:08:42,671
So you can count the rank with rows or with columns.

123
00:08:43,096 --> 00:08:48,442
In practice, a computer finds it for you, as we will see in the Python demo.

124
00:08:50,173 --> 00:08:52,048
Why does this matter in machine learning?

125
00:08:52,486 --> 00:08:54,907
Look at this toy table of three objects.

126
00:08:55,359 --> 00:08:57,978
The first column is the length in meters.

127
00:08:58,417 --> 00:09:04,401
The second column is the same length in centimeters, which is one hundred times the first column.

128
00:09:04,827 --> 00:09:07,752
The third column is the weight in kilograms.

129
00:09:08,673 --> 00:09:13,034
There are three columns, but the second one is a multiple of the first.

130
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:15,907
So the rank is two, not three.

131
00:09:16,332 --> 00:09:18,792
The data has only two real directions.

132
00:09:19,191 --> 00:09:25,295
A redundant feature like this adds no new information, and finding the rank helps us detect it.

133
00:09:26,793 --> 00:09:35,223
Rank is also behind a popular method for fine-tuning large language models, called LoRA, which stands for low-rank adaptation.

134
00:09:35,649 --> 00:09:43,854
LoRA lets each weight matrix change only along a few directions, for example sixteen out of seven hundred sixty-eight.

135
00:09:44,332 --> 00:09:47,471
Fewer directions means far fewer numbers to train.

136
00:09:47,883 --> 00:09:48,894
We only mention it here.

137
00:09:50,419 --> 00:09:51,496
Where does all this come from?

138
00:09:51,922 --> 00:10:01,350
Independence, basis, dimension and rank are all questions about linear combinations: which points can we reach by adding and scaling?

139
00:10:01,776 --> 00:10:05,778
So this whole video rests on axiom A, linearity.

140
00:10:07,473 --> 00:10:07,991
Your turn.

141
00:10:08,430 --> 00:10:10,172
Pause the video and try this.

142
00:10:10,598 --> 00:10:15,544
Are the vectors one, two and three, six linearly independent?

143
00:10:15,983 --> 00:10:21,502
If not, find weights, not all zero, that combine them into the zero vector.

144
00:10:22,479 --> 00:10:24,979
Three, six is three times one, two.

145
00:10:25,405 --> 00:10:26,375
So they are dependent.

146
00:10:26,814 --> 00:10:29,886
One choice of weights is three and minus one.

147
00:10:30,351 --> 00:10:36,402
Three times one, two is three, six, and minus three, six cancels it.

148
00:10:36,881 --> 00:10:41,841
Three times the first vector minus the second gives zero, zero.

149
00:10:43,373 --> 00:10:44,197
Second question.

150
00:10:44,623 --> 00:10:51,285
What is the rank of the two by three matrix with rows one, two, three and two, four, six?

151
00:10:51,684 --> 00:10:55,168
Count the independent columns, or the independent rows.

152
00:10:55,593 --> 00:10:56,285
Pause and try.

153
00:10:57,206 --> 00:11:00,118
The second row is two times the first row.

154
00:11:00,557 --> 00:11:04,626
So there is only one independent row, and the rank is one.

155
00:11:05,065 --> 00:11:09,573
The columns agree: one, two, then two, four, then three, six.

156
00:11:10,012 --> 00:11:12,206
They are all multiples of one, two.

157
00:11:12,631 --> 00:11:15,437
One independent column, so the rank is one.

158
00:11:17,173 --> 00:11:18,476
Let's check it in NumPy.

159
00:11:18,915 --> 00:11:22,771
The function matrix rank counts the independent columns for us.

160
00:11:23,210 --> 00:11:34,194
We test a, b and c, solve for the coordinates of one, three in the new basis, and find the rank of our three examples and of the toy table.

161
00:11:35,739 --> 00:11:39,582
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

162
00:11:39,995 --> 00:11:46,258
A plus b minus c is the zero vector, and the rank of a, b and c is two.

163
00:11:46,697 --> 00:11:49,768
The coordinates in the new basis are two and one.

164
00:11:50,194 --> 00:11:53,838
The ranks of the examples are one, two and three.

165
00:11:54,276 --> 00:11:56,098
And the toy table has rank two.

166
00:11:57,839 --> 00:11:58,491
In summary.

167
00:11:58,930 --> 00:12:05,539
Vectors are linearly independent when the only way to combine them into zero is with all-zero weights.

168
00:12:05,964 --> 00:12:09,728
A basis is an independent set that spans the space.

169
00:12:10,153 --> 00:12:16,390
Every point has unique coordinates in a basis, and the coordinates change when the basis changes.

170
00:12:17,293 --> 00:12:20,790
Every basis has the same size: the dimension.

171
00:12:21,215 --> 00:12:27,253
The rank of a matrix is the number of independent columns, which equals the number of independent rows.

172
00:12:27,665 --> 00:12:33,955
Next, in video four: linear mappings, the functions that respect adding and scaling.

173
00:12:34,394 --> 00:12:34,939
See you there.

174
00:12:36,873 --> 00:12:38,694
This video was made by David R.

175
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:42,365
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

176
00:12:42,923 --> 00:12:43,641
Thanks for watching.
